main/java/exercises06/LockFreeStack.java.
Explain why those linearization points show
that the implementation of the Treiber Stack is correct.
For the explanation, follow a similar approach as we
did in lecture 6 for the MS Queue (slides 34 and 35).

linearization point for Push:
H1: (!top.compareAndSet(oldHead,newHead));

because this is when the new node is successfully
pushed onto the stack and becomes visible to other threads.

linearization points for Pop:
P1:  oldHead = top.get();
P2: (!top.compareAndSet(oldHead,newHead));

because that is when the stack has been successfully
updated and the old head has been removed.


Correctness explanation:
-Example: thread x push onto the stack concurrently with other threads.
Either x succeeds in pushing with operation H1,
or it fails because another thread succeeded in doing so and x retries.

-Example: thread x pops from the stack with plenty elements
concurrently with other threads.
x gets the head of the stack with operation P1. If the head is not empty
either x will succeed in popping with operation P2,
or it fails because another thread succeeded in doing so
and x retries by getting the new head with P1.

-Example: Same as previous example but the stack may run out of elements:
When x gets the head with P1 and it receives null,
it means the stack was empty at the time and x correctly returns null.



