Exercise 6.3 In this exercise, we revisit the progress notions for
non-blocking computation that we discussed
in lecture 5.
1. Consider the reader-writer locks exercise from week 5.
There are four methods included in this type of
locks: writerTryLock, writerUnlock, readerTryLock and readerUnlock. State, for each
method, whether they are wait-free, lock-free or
obstruction-free and explain your answers.


writerTryLock(): Wait-free
This method uses a single CAS operation.
It either succeeds or fails in a single attempt.
This method completes in a bounded number of steps (just one CAS operation)
regardless of the actions of other threads. It's guaranteed to complete in finite time.

writerUnlock(): Wait-free <- CORRECT
This method checks the current state and uses a single CAS operation.
It either succeeds or throws an exception in a single attempt.
Like writerTryLock(), this method completes in a bounded number of steps
(one check and one CAS operation) regardless of other threads' actions.
WRONG ->(Alternative answer: Lock-free, because holders.get might not succeed in a single
attempt, because it is an atomic reference, which itself under the hood
utilizes a do-while loop, which might need to retry multiple times)

readerTryLock(): Lock-free
This method uses a do-while loop with compareAndSet (CAS) operation.
It can retry multiple times if there's contention.
It returns false if a writer holds the lock, without retrying.
The method will eventually complete (either succeed or fail)
regardless of the actions of other threads. It doesn't use blocking operations,
but it may need to retry, so it's not wait-free.

readerUnlock(): Lock-free
This method uses a do-while loop with CAS operation.
It can retry multiple times if there's contention.
It throws an exception if the thread doesn't hold a read lock.
Similar to readerTryLock().
It may need to retry an unknown amount of times, so it's not wait-free.
However, if the a given thread fails and tries again it must be because of the
another thread made progress meaning it is lock-free,
because that other thread completed in a finite number of steps.



Exercise 6.4 This exercise is a slightly modified
version of exercise 3.7 in Herlihy page 71. This exercise
concerns the following lock-free queue implementation:
class IQueue<T> {
AtomicInteger head = new AtomicInteger(0);
AtomicInteger tail = new AtomicInteger(0);
T[] items = (T[]) new Object[Integer.MAX_VALUE];
public void enq(T x) {
int slot;
do {
slot = tail.get();
} while (!tail.compareAndSet(slot, slot+1)); // E4
items[slot] = x;
}
public T deq() throws EmptyException {
T value;
int slot;
do {
slot = head.get();
value = items[slot];
if (value == null)
throw new EmptyException();
} while (!head.compareAndSet(slot, slot+1)); // D8
return value;
}
}
1. In the code, we have marked two linearization points
E4 and D8 for enq and deq, respectively. Show that,
with these linearization points, the implementation is
not linearizable (according to the standard specification
of a sequential FIFO queue).

Simple version:
We enqueue some item x.
We reach linearization point E4 (and waits)
If specification holds, then x should be visible to all other threads.
So another thread dequeues x, but gets EmptyException.
This shouldn't be possible!
Therefore the implementation is not linearizable.

Longer version:
Consider two threads T1 and T2 executing the following operations concurrently:
T1: enq(A)
T2: enq(B)
T1: deq()
T2: deq()
Now, let's consider a possible interleaving of these operations:

T1 begins enq(A), reaches E4 (CAS succeeds)
T2 begins enq(B), reaches E4 (CAS succeeds)
T1 completes enq(A) by writing A to items[slot]
T1 begins deq(), reads head (0), reads value A, reaches D8 (CAS succeeds)
T2 completes enq(B) by writing B to items[slot+1]
T2 begins deq(), reads head (1), reads value null, throws EmptyException

According to the given linearization points (E4 for enq and D8 for deq),
the linearization order would be:
<enq(A), enq(B), deq():A, deq():EmptyException>
However, this is not a valid sequential history for a queue.
After two enqueue operations and one successful dequeue,
the queue should not be empty.
This shows that the given linearization points do not
produce a correct linearizable implementation


Exercise 6.5 Your task in this exercise is to reason about
whether the following program is correctly synchronized;
according to the definition of correctly synchronized
program in the Java memory model.
Lock l = new ReentrantLock();
Thread one = new Thread(() -> {
l.lock(); // (1)
a=1; // (2)
x=b; // (3)
l.unlock(); // (4)
}).start();
Thread two = new Thread(() -> {
l.lock(); // (5)
b=1; // (6)
y=a; // (7)
l.unlock(); // (8)
}).start();

1. Prove (formally) whether the program above is or is not
correctly synchronized. To this end, recall the
definitions of correctly synchronized program, data race,
execution, synchronization order, happens-before,
etc. that we discussed in lecture 6. You may use the numbers
in the comments at the end of the lines to refer
to the operations in the program.

let's consider all shared variables (a, b, x, y) and their accesses:
Variable a: Written in (2), read in (7)
Variable b: Written in (6), read in (3)
Variable x: Written in (3)
Variable y: Written in (7)

For each pair of conflicting accesses:

(2) and (7): These are ordered by the happens-before relationship
established by the lock operations. Either (1-4) happens-before (5-8), or vice versa.
(6) and (3): Similarly, these are ordered by the lock operations.

Therefore, all conflicting accesses are ordered by happens-before
relationships, which means there are no data races in this program.
Conclusion: The program is correctly synchronized according to the Java Memory Mode